index 40f50829c575fa4f6f232145117f7f333ade995a..e83d52c40f7b4a1be227193fd414b4d627471331 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -12,4 +12,26 @@ An empty pointer returns the document itself. Member names use `~0` for `~` and
 including the array token `-`. Pass a plain pointer string, not a URI fragment
 such as `#/items/0`; percent escapes are treated literally.
 
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an array of JSON Patch operations as defined
+by [RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902.html). It supports `add`,
+`remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, and `test`, applying them in order and returning
+a new value without mutating the document or patch. Invalid patches, unresolved
+references, and failed tests throw errors; the original inputs remain unchanged
+even on failure.
+
+```ts
+applyPatch({ items: ['first'] }, [
+  { op: 'add', path: '/items/-', value: 'second' },
+  { op: 'test', path: '/items/0', value: 'first' },
+]); // { items: ['first', 'second'] }
+```
+
+The empty path addresses the entire document. Removing it returns `undefined`,
+representing an absent document; a subsequent `add` at the empty path can restore
+it. Other operations require an existing document. Array additions insert at an
+index up to the array length, or append with `-`; other array references require
+an existing index. `test` compares JSON values structurally, ignoring object key
+order. Documents and operation values must be JSON values, with finite numbers
+and no cycles.
+
 Run tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or newer, with native TypeScript support).
